DNS:
When we purchase a DNS in godaddy / hostinger, godaddy updates the info to corresponding TLD (.com or .in or .online etc). Then TLD updates to root servers. Then root servers provide name servers to TLD. TLD provides those name servers to hostinger.
We can see name servers for our host in godaddy/hostinger.
We create a A record in godaddy/hostinger with the IP address. A record is responsible to provide IP for your domain name.
Root servers provide name servers. Name servers gives A record which we updated.





When we try a DNS from browser the flow is as follows:
Browser->Cache->OS->OS cache -> ISP DNS resolver ->root server ->TLD information -> TLD name servers -> name servers-> records
A -> IP address
CNAME -> another domain redirect
MX – mail servers
NS – name servers
SOA – authorization
TXT – validation purpose

When we book a domain, domain registrars update name servers for that domain from TLD (top level domain).
Book a domain in domain registrar -> domain registrar updates name servers to TLD ->TLD updates name servers to root servers.
If we change IP in A record, then update will goto TLD. But if we update name servers then only the update will goto root servers.
If we transfer the domain name from one registrar (hostingger) to another registar (AWS Route53), then name servers will change. 
1.	Create a domain in hostinger 
2.	Create a hosted zone in AWS route 53. Give the same domain name in route53
3.	Then AWS shows you 4 name sevrers.
4.	Update these 4 name servers in Hostinger. It may take upto 24 hours to update. Now hostinger updates the name servers to TLD. TLD updates the changed name servers to root servers.

Update A record with IP address of front end server.
Create another A record by entering subdomain for backend and DB server private IPs.


